(function () {
  function P(q, a, h, s) { return { q: q, a: a, h: h || "", s: s || "" }; }

  var SSC_JE_ENC_FLUIDS = {};
SSC_JE_ENC_FLUIDS.fluids = [
      P('What is density of a fluid and its unit?', 'Mass per unit volume; SI unit kg/m³.', 'ρ = m/V.', 'Density ρ = m/V. Water at 4 °C has a density of 1000 kg/m³. It appears in the continuity equation ρAV, in momentum analysis and in the Reynolds number. Relative density compares ρ with that of water at 4 °C.'),
      P('Define specific weight and give its value for water.', 'Weight per unit volume γ = ρg; for water ≈ 9810 N/m³.', 'γ = ρ × 9.81.', 'γ = ρg. For water, 1000 × 9.81 = 9810 N/m³. Specific weight converts head into pressure: p = γh. It is also called weight density.'),
      P('What is specific gravity of a fluid?', 'Ratio of density of the fluid to that of water at 4 °C; dimensionless.', 's = ρ/ρ(water).', 'Mercury has s = 13.6, so ρ = 13600 kg/m³ and γ = 133416 N/m³. Manometer equations are written with the term S × γw × h, and it is also called relative density.'),
      P('Define dynamic viscosity and give its unit.', 'Shear stress per unit velocity gradient; SI unit Pa·s or N·s/m².', 'μ = τ/(du/dy).', '1 poise = 0.1 Pa·s and 1 Pa·s = 1 N·s/m². For Newtonian fluids τ = μ(du/dy). Higher μ means the fluid resists shearing motion more strongly.'),
      P('What is kinematic viscosity?', 'Ratio of dynamic viscosity to density ν = μ/ρ; unit m²/s.', 'ν = μ/ρ.', '1 stoke = 10⁻⁴ m²/s. It is used in the Reynolds number Re = VD/ν. Water at 20 °C has ν ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁶ m²/s, equal to 1 centistoke.'),
      P('What is a Newtonian fluid?', 'A fluid for which shear stress is directly proportional to the velocity gradient.', 'τ ∝ du/dy.', 'The slope of the τ versus du/dy plot is constant and equals μ. Water, air and most oils obey the Newton law of viscosity; their viscosity changes only with temperature and pressure.'),
      P('Name the non-Newtonian fluid classes.', 'Bingham plastic, pseudo-plastic and dilatant fluids; also thixotropic and rheopectic.', 'τ does not vary linearly with du/dy.', 'Modelled by τ = A(du/dy)ⁿ: n < 1 pseudo-plastic (paints, polymer melts), n > 1 dilatant (quicksand, starch slurries). A Bingham plastic needs a finite yield stress before flowing (toothpaste, drilling mud).'),
      P('What is an ideal fluid?', 'A fluid having zero viscosity and zero compressibility.', 'No shear stress and no volume change.', 'It is inviscid and incompressible, so shear stresses are always zero. It is used to derive the Euler equation, the Bernoulli equation and velocity-potential theory for frictionless flow.'),
      P('What is a real fluid?', 'A fluid that possesses viscosity and compressibility.', 'Applies to all actual fluids.', 'Real liquids and gases dissipate energy through viscous action. Their analysis requires friction factors, boundary-layer concepts and energy-loss terms in the flow equations.'),
      P('Define bulk modulus of a liquid.', 'Ratio of pressure change to volumetric strain: K = −V(dp/dV).', 'Unit is Pa or N/m².', 'For water K ≈ 2.0–2.2 × 10⁹ Pa (about 2 GPa). A higher K means lower compressibility. It also gives the sound velocity in the liquid: c = √(K/ρ).'),
      P('What is compressibility of a fluid?', 'Reciprocal of bulk modulus, 1/K; the volumetric strain per unit pressure rise.', 'β = 1/K.', 'Gases are far more compressible than liquids, hence they have a much larger compressibility. The property is central to water-hammer and speed-of-sound calculations.'),
      P('Define surface tension and state its unit.', 'Tensile force acting on unit length of the liquid surface; unit N/m.', 'σ = F/L.', 'It arises from unbalanced cohesive forces at the free surface. For the water–air interface at 20 °C, σ ≈ 0.073 N/m. It explains droplet pressure, capillary rise and bubble pressure.'),
      P('What is the pressure inside a liquid droplet?', 'Excess pressure Δp = 4σ/d.', 'Δp = 4σ/d.', 'Resolving forces on a half-sphere: πr²Δp = 2πrσ gives Δp = 2σ/r = 4σ/d, where d is the droplet diameter. Smaller droplets carry a larger internal excess pressure.'),
      P('What is the excess pressure inside a soap bubble?', 'Δp = 8σ/d.', 'A bubble has two surfaces.', 'A soap bubble has inner and outer surfaces, each contributing 2σ/r, so Δp = 4σ/r = 8σ/d. A single-surface liquid droplet gives only half of this value.'),
      P('What is the excess pressure in a liquid jet?', 'Δp = 2σ/d.', 'One curved surface only.', 'For a cylindrical jet only one curved surface carries tension, giving Δp = σ/r = 2σ/d. This is half the droplet value and a quarter of the soap-bubble value.'),
      P('Write the formula for capillary rise in a small tube.', 'h = 4σ cosθ/(ρgd) = 4σ cosθ/(γd).', 'h = 4σcosθ/γd.', 'Balance the weight of the risen column against the surface pull: πdσ cosθ = (πd²/4)γh. Water with θ < 90° rises; mercury with θ > 90° is depressed because cosθ is negative. The effect diminishes with tube diameter.'),
      P('Define vapour pressure of a liquid.', 'Pressure exerted by the vapour in equilibrium with its liquid at a given temperature.', 'Increases sharply with temperature.', 'Water at 20 °C has a vapour pressure of about 2.34 kPa absolute. Boiling begins when the local absolute pressure falls to the vapour pressure, the onset condition of cavitation.'),
      P('What is cavitation in fluids?', 'Formation and violent collapse of vapour bubbles when the local pressure drops below the vapour pressure.', 'Pressure < p(vapour).', 'Bubbles form where pressure is low and implode in higher-pressure regions. The collapse shocks pit metal surfaces in pumps, turbines, draft tubes and valves, and also lower efficiency.'),
      P('How is cavitation generally prevented?', 'Keep the operating pressure above the vapour pressure of the liquid.', 'Raise pressure or cool the liquid.', 'Practically this means deeper submergence of the machine, a smaller suction lift, lower speed, avoiding sharp throttles and using cavitation-resistant materials such as stainless steel.'),
      P('What does a barometer measure?', 'Atmospheric pressure using a mercury column.', '760 mm Hg equals 1 atmosphere.', '1 atm = 1.013 bar = 760 mm Hg = 10.33 m of water. The barometric height is h = p(atm)/(ρHg × g), so a standard mercury barometer stands about 760 mm high.'),
      P('What is a piezometer?', 'A simple vertical tube open to the atmosphere that measures gauge pressure p = γh.', 'p = ρgh.', 'The liquid rises to a height equal to the pressure head. It suits only liquids at moderate pressure; it cannot be used for gases or for large or negative pressures because the column becomes too long.'),
      P('Give the pressure relation of a U-tube manometer with a heavy liquid.', 'For a gas: p = S γw h; with mercury, p = 13.6 × 9810 × h.', 'p = Sm γw h.', 'S is the relative density of the manometric liquid and h the column difference. When a liquid of relative density Sf is gauged, the term Sf γw times the liquid column length is subtracted.'),
      P('What does a differential manometer measure?', 'The pressure difference between two points or sections.', 'A U-tube connects the two taps.', 'Used across orifices, venturimeters and filters, it reads Δp = (Sm − Sf)γw h. The same head h gives the discharge through a meter or the resistance of a filter element.'),
      P('Why is an inclined manometer used?', 'To measure very small pressure differences accurately.', 'h = L sinθ.', 'The tube is inclined so the scale length L along the tube is much longer than the vertical rise h, magnifying the reading and improving sensitivity. It is standard for pitot and draft measurements.'),
      P('Describe a Bourdon gauge.', 'A pressure gauge in which a curved elastic tube straightens under internal pressure and moves a pointer.', 'Mechanical; reads gauge pressure.', 'The Bourdon tube deflects in proportion to gauge pressure and drives a link-and-gear mechanism. It is robust and cheap but needs calibration and is not accurate at very low pressures or under vibration.'),
      P('Relate gauge, absolute and vacuum pressure.', 'p(absolute) = p(gauge) + p(atm); vacuum = p(atm) − p(absolute).', 'Gauge pressure is relative to atmosphere.', 'Absolute pressures must be used wherever vapour pressure and cavitation are involved. Pressure below the local atmosphere is often expressed as a vacuum, that is, a negative gauge pressure.'),
      P('State Pascal law.', 'In a confined static fluid, pressure acts equally in all directions and is transmitted undiminished.', 'Basis of the hydraulic press and lift.', 'Any change of pressure at one point in a confined incompressible fluid is felt unchanged at every point, and the pressure always acts normal to any surface in contact with the fluid.'),
      P('How is force multiplied in a hydraulic lift?', 'F2 = F1 × (A2/A1), with the same pressure on both pistons.', 'p = F/A.', 'Example: F1 = 100 N on area A1 = 0.01 m² gives p = 10 kPa; on A2 = 1 m² it lifts F2 = 10,000 N. The stroke is reduced in the same ratio because volume is conserved.'),
      P('State the hydrostatic law.', 'The rate of increase of pressure with depth equals the specific weight: dp/dh = ρg.', 'dp = γ dh.', 'In a static incompressible liquid, pressure increases linearly with depth and p = p0 + ρgh. This law underlies all total-pressure computations on submerged surfaces.'),
      P('How does pressure vary with depth in a static liquid?', 'Linearly: p = p0 + ρgh, and it is the same on any horizontal plane.', 'p = ρgh gauge.', 'Points at equal level in a connected static liquid carry equal pressure, which allows manometer balancing. The pressure at the base of a tank is p = γh regardless of the tank shape.'),
      P('What is total pressure on an immersed plane surface?', 'F = γ A h̄, where A is the wetted area and h̄ the depth of its centroid.', 'γ A h̄.', 'It is the product of the pressure at the centroid and the area. For a given area and centroid depth the result is independent of the inclination of the plane.'),
      P('Define centre of pressure.', 'The point on the surface where the resultant hydrostatic force acts.', 'It lies below the centroid of a submerged plane.', 'For a plane at angle θ, h(cp) = h̄ + (I_G sin²θ)/(A h̄), where I_G is the second moment of the area about a horizontal centroidal axis. For a horizontal surface the two coincide.'),
      P('Total pressure and CP for a vertical rectangular plate with its top at the surface.', 'F = γbd²/2 acting at 2d/3 below the surface; b = width, d = depth.', 'Pressure distribution is triangular.', 'Pressure varies linearly from zero at the surface to γd at the bottom. F = γ(bd)(d/2) = γbd²/2, and the centroid of the pressure triangle, the CP, sits at 2d/3 depth.'),
      P('Where lies the CP of an inclined submerged plane?', 'h(cp) = h̄ + I_G sin²θ/(A h̄) measured along the plane.', 'The sin²θ term decreases with tilt.', 'The increment over the centroid depth comes from the non-uniform pressure. As the plane approaches horizontal, sin²θ tends to zero and the CP moves toward the centroid.'),
      P('Give the depth of CP for a vertical circular plate tangent to the free surface.', 'At 5D/8 below the surface, D being the diameter.', 'h̄ + I/Ah̄.', 'h̄ = D/2, A = πD²/4 and I = πD⁴/64 give an offset of D/8, hence the total depth is D/2 + D/8 = 5D/8.'),
      P('What is the total pressure on a vertical sluice gate between water depths h1 and h2?', 'F = γb(h1² − h2²)/2 where b is the gate width.', 'Difference of two triangles.', 'The upstream face sees a triangle of height h1 and the downstream face sees one of height h2. The resultant is the difference γb(h1² − h2²)/2, whose CP is below the centroid of the difference area.'),
      P('How are the forces on a curved submerged surface found?', 'Horizontal component = force on its vertical projection; vertical component = weight of liquid above it.', 'Resolve by projections.', 'Fx = γ × area of the vertical projection × centroid depth, and Fy = γ × volume of liquid above the surface. Resultant F = √(Fx² + Fy²) passes through the centre of the affected area.'),
      P('What is the total pressure on a vertical dam wall holding water to height H?', 'F = γbH²/2 acting at H/3 above the base.', 'Triangular distribution, width b.', 'For H = 10 m and b = 1 m, F = 9810 × 1 × 100/2 = 490,500 N = 490.5 kN per metre width, acting 3.33 m above the base. The CP is one-third of the height above the bottom.'),
      P('Find the total pressure and CP on a 3 m deep, 2 m wide vertical plate with its top at the surface.', 'F = 88,290 N ≈ 88.29 kN acting at 2.0 m depth.', 'F = γbd²/2 = γAh̄.', 'Area A = 6 m² and centroid depth h̄ = 1.5 m, so F = γAh̄ = 9810 × 6 × 1.5 = 88,290 N. The CP is at 2d/3 = 2.0 m below the free surface.'),
      P('State Archimedes principle.', 'The upthrust on a submerged or floating body equals the weight of the displaced liquid.', 'Fb = γ × displaced volume.', 'For a floating body the upthrust equals its own weight. The principle underlies ships, submarines, hydrometers and balloons, and is used to find buoyant forces.'),
      P('When does a body float, sink or hover in a liquid?', 'Float when its mean density is less than the liquid, sink when greater, and remain in equilibrium when equal.', 'Compare the two densities.', 'In floating equilibrium the body displaces its own weight of liquid, so the submerged fraction equals ρ(body)/ρ(liquid). Hovering requires equal mean densities.'),
      P('What fraction of a floating body remains submerged?', 'The fraction equals the ratio of the mean density of the body to the density of the liquid.', 'Fraction = ρ(body)/ρ(liquid).', 'Example: a block of density 600 kg/m³ floating in water has 60% of its volume submerged, because the buoyant force 1000g × 0.6V balances the weight 600gV.'),
      P('Define metacentre.', 'The intersection of the vertical through the tilted centre of buoyancy with the original vertical axis through the centre of gravity.', 'Denoted by M.', 'For small angles of heel, M is effectively fixed. The distance GM, the metacentric height, decides the stability and the natural rolling period of a floating body.'),
      P('Give the metacentric height formula.', 'GM = BM − BG when B lies below G; BM = I/V.', 'GM = BM ± BG.', 'I is the second moment of the water-plane area about the axis of tilt and V the displaced volume. When the centre of buoyancy lies above G the sign reverses: GM = BM + BG.'),
      P('When is a floating body stable?', 'When its metacentre M lies above the centre of gravity G.', 'Righting couple W × GM × sinθ.', 'A tilt then produces a righting couple W × GM × sinθ that restores the upright position. If M falls below G the couple capsizes the body. Neutral stability occurs when M coincides with G.'),
      P('When is a fully submerged body stable?', 'When its centre of buoyancy B lies above its centre of gravity G.', 'Submerged: metacentre coincides with B.', 'For a completely submerged body the metacentre is fixed at the centre of buoyancy, so stability requires B above G. A submarine keeps B above G by adjusting the ballast tanks.'),
      P('Compute the upthrust on a 0.4 m cube fully immersed in water.', 'Fb = γV = 9810 × 0.064 = 627.84 N.', 'Fb = γV.', 'Displaced volume V = 0.4³ = 0.064 m³, so Fb = 9810 × 0.064 = 627.84 N acting upward through the centroid. If the cube weighs 627.84 N it stays fully immersed in equilibrium.'),
      P('Name the three equilibrium states of a floating body.', 'Stable, neutral and unstable equilibrium.', 'Position of M relative to G decides the state.', 'Stable: M above G; neutral: M coincides with G; unstable: M below G. For small oscillations the rolling period is about T = 2π√(k²/(g·GM)), with k the radius of gyration about the rotation axis.'),
      P('How does the free surface behave in a horizontally accelerating tank?', 'It slopes as a plane with tanθ = a/g, where a is the acceleration.', 'Surface remains plane.', 'The liquid moves like a rigid body with no relative motion. Pressure at any point is p = ρ(ax + gy), and the free surface follows a·x + g·y = constant.'),
      P('How does pressure change in a tank accelerating vertically?', 'p = γh(1 + a/g) for upward acceleration and p = γh(1 − a/g) for downward acceleration.', 'Effective gravity is g ± a.', 'The isobars remain horizontal because the acceleration is vertical. In free fall a = g gives zero pressure everywhere. This belongs to the relative equilibrium of liquids.'),
      P('What is the free surface shape in a vessel rotating at uniform angular speed ω?', 'A paraboloid of revolution: z = ω²r²/(2g).', 'z = ω²r²/2g.', 'The liquid rises above the vertex as r², forming a paraboloid, the classic forced-vortex surface. In a closed rotating vessel the pressure follows p = p0 + ω²r²ρ/2 − ρgz.'),
      P('Distinguish Eulerian and Lagrangian descriptions of flow.', 'Eulerian observes the field at fixed points in space; Lagrangian follows individual fluid particles.', 'Field versus particle description.', 'Engineers usually take the Eulerian viewpoint, dealing with velocity and pressure at fixed points. The Lagrangian approach is used in particle-path and streak-line studies.'),
      P('What is steady flow?', 'Flow in which conditions at any point do not change with time.', '∂/∂t = 0.', 'Velocity, pressure and density may vary from point to point, but at each fixed point they remain constant. In steady flow, streamlines, path lines and streak lines all coincide.'),
      P('What is uniform flow?', 'Flow in which the velocity does not change along the flow direction at a given instant.', '∂V/∂s = 0.', 'Uniform flow may still be unsteady. Flow far from a pipe entrance approaches uniform conditions; non-uniform flow occurs at contractions, bends, diffusers and channel transitions.'),
      P('Define path, stream and streak lines.', 'Path line is the locus of one particle over time; streamline is tangent to the velocity at an instant; streak line passes through all particles that went through a fixed point.', 'In steady flow the three coincide.', 'Streamlines never cross. A passage between two adjacent streamlines conveys a constant discharge per unit width in steady two-dimensional flow.'),
      P('Write the continuity equation for fluid flow.', 'ρ1A1V1 = ρ2A2V2; for incompressible flow A1V1 = A2V2 = Q.', 'Mass is conserved.', 'In differential form, div(ρV) + ∂ρ/∂t = 0. Along with momentum and energy balances it is one of the three governing equations of fluid mechanics.'),
      P('Define the stream function ψ.', 'A two-dimensional flow function with u = ∂ψ/∂y and v = −∂ψ/∂x; ψ is constant along a streamline.', 'u = ∂ψ/∂y, v = −∂ψ/∂x.', 'The difference of ψ between two streamlines equals the volume rate of flow per unit width between them. Any real two-dimensional flow has a stream function because its definition enforces continuity.'),
      P('What is the velocity potential φ?', 'A function whose negative gradient equals the velocity: u = −∂φ/∂x, v = −∂φ/∂y.', 'Exists only for irrotational flow.', 'Lines of constant φ are equipotential lines. Because the curl of a gradient is zero, the existence of φ guarantees irrotationality and leads to the Laplace equation.'),
      P('Which equation does the velocity potential satisfy?', 'The Laplace equation: ∂²φ/∂x² + ∂²φ/∂y² = 0.', 'Irrotational and incompressible flow.', 'Substituting u and v from φ into continuity gives the Laplace equation. For irrotational flow the stream function obeys the same equation.'),
      P('What is the relation between streamlines and equipotential lines?', 'They intersect at right angles, forming the flow net.', 'Cauchy–Riemann conditions.', 'From u = ∂ψ/∂y = −∂φ/∂x and v = −∂ψ/∂x = −∂φ/∂y, the two families are mutually orthogonal everywhere in irrotational two-dimensional flow.'),
      P('Define circulation Γ.', 'The line integral of tangential velocity around a closed curve: Γ = ∮ V cosθ ds.', 'Γ = ∮ Vs ds.', 'For irrotational flow Γ is zero around any closed curve in the fluid. Finite circulation around a body produces lift, as in the Kutta–Joukowski relation L = ρVΓ.'),
      P('What are rotational and irrotational flows?', 'Irrotational flow has zero vorticity; rotational flow has non-zero vorticity.', 'ω = ∇ × V.', 'For two-dimensional flow, ωz = ∂v/∂x − ∂u/∂y. Zero vorticity is the condition for the existence of the velocity potential. Viscous boundary-layer flow is rotational; frictionless free-stream flow is usually irrotational.'),
      P('What is a flow net?', 'An orthogonal mesh of streamlines and equipotential lines for irrotational two-dimensional flow.', 'Cells are nearly square in an ideal net.', 'Velocity is inversely proportional to the spacing between streamlines, and equal potential drops occur across cells. A good flow net gives quick estimates of velocity and pressure around bodies, gates and weirs.'),
      P('Write the Euler equation of motion along a streamline.', 'dp/ρ + VdV + gdz = 0.', 'Valid for an ideal fluid along a streamline.', 'It neglects viscosity. Integrating it for steady incompressible flow gives the Bernoulli equation; for isentropic gas flow it gives the pressure–velocity relation of gas dynamics.'),
      P('Write the Bernoulli equation and name its terms.', 'p/γ + V²/2g + z = constant.', 'Pressure + velocity + elevation heads.', 'Each term is a metre of head: pressure head p/γ, velocity head V²/2g and datum head z. The equation states that the total mechanical head is constant along a streamline for ideal steady incompressible flow.'),
      P('State the assumptions of the Bernoulli equation.', 'Flow is steady, incompressible and inviscid along a streamline, with no energy added or removed.', 'Five idealisations.', 'Real flows require energy-loss terms: between two sections hf is subtracted and pump or turbine heads are added, otherwise the total head would only appear constant.'),
      P('Write the venturimeter discharge formula.', 'Q = Cd a1 a2 √(2gh)/√(a1² − a2²), with Cd ≈ 0.95–0.99.', 'a = throat area.', 'h is the differential head between inlet and throat from the converging section. The smooth divergence recovers most of the pressure, which is why the losses and the discharge coefficient are small.'),
      P('Compare an orifice meter with a venturimeter.', 'The orifice meter is cheaper but has Cd ≈ 0.62 and a large permanent loss; the venturimeter has Cd ≈ 0.98.', 'Cd 0.62 versus 0.98.', 'The orifice plate forms a vena contracta and a wake that dissipates energy, so its discharge coefficient is low. The venturimeter is preferred when metering losses matter.'),
      P('How does a pitot tube measure velocity?', 'V = √(2gh), where h is the stagnation minus static head.', 'Kinetic energy into pressure head.', 'The stagnation pressure is p + ρV²/2 while the static port gives p. Their difference, the dynamic head h, gives V = √(2gh). Combined pitot-static tubes are used in aircraft and pipelines.'),
      P('What is the function of a nozzle in a fluid system?', 'To convert pressure energy into kinetic energy, accelerating the fluid.', 'Head into velocity.', 'V = Cv√(2gH) with Cv ≈ 0.95–0.98 for real nozzles. Nozzles are used in Pelton turbines, fire hoses, jet engines and metering systems.'),
      P('Explain the working of a siphon and its height limit.', 'Liquid flows over a crest by atmospheric pressure while the pipe stays full; the summit head must stay above the vapour-pressure head.', 'Limiting lift near one atmosphere of head.', 'Atmospheric pressure pushes the liquid up the short leg. The theoretical maximum lift is about 10.33 m; practically near 8 m because approaching the vapour pressure causes column breakage and cavitation.'),
      P('Define hydraulic gradient line and total energy gradient line.', 'HGL is the level of p/γ + z; EGL is HGL plus the velocity head V²/2g.', 'EGL lies above HGL.', 'In a pipeline with friction both lines slope downward in the flow direction. Piezometers show the HGL, a pitot tube shows the EGL; the gap between them is V²/2g.'),
      P('Write the impulse–momentum equation.', 'F = ρQ(V2 − V1), the net force on a control volume.', 'Newton second law in flux form.', 'The force on the fluid equals the rate of change of its momentum. It is used for jet impact on vanes, pipe bends and nozzles, cases where Bernoulli alone cannot give the boundary forces.'),
      P('What are the momentum and kinetic-energy correction factors?', 'β = (1/AV²)∫u²dA and α = (1/AV³)∫u³dA across the section.', 'β for momentum, α for energy.', 'For laminar pipe flow α = 2 and β = 4/3. In turbulent flow both are near unity and usually neglected. They correct the one-dimensional momentum and energy forms for the real velocity profile.'),
      P('What is the force of a jet on a stationary vertical flat plate?', 'F = ρAV² acting along the jet axis.', 'F = ρAV².', 'The jet is brought to rest forward, so F = ρQ(V − 0) = ρAV². This is the fundamental result of impact-of-jet on vane analyses.'),
      P('What is the force on a flat plate moving away from the jet at speed u?', 'F = ρA(V − u)².', 'Use the relative velocity V − u.', 'The plate recedes, so the effective impact velocity is V − u and the relative discharge A(V − u) strikes it. Work per second is Fu = ρA(V − u)²u, which is maximum when u = V/3.'),
      P('What is the force of a jet on an inclined stationary plate at angle θ?', 'F = ρAV² sinθ normal to the plate.', 'Only the normal component is destroyed.', 'The tangential component of the jet leaves parallel to the plate without transferring normal momentum. Hence the normal force is ρAV² sinθ.'),
      P('State the work of a jet on a series of moving curved vanes.', 'W = ρQ(Vw1 − Vw2)u; for ideal vanes deflecting the jet by 180°, W = 2ρAVu(V − u), maximum when u = V/2.', 'Use whirl velocities and bucket speed.', 'A series of vanes intercepts the whole jet, so Q = AV; with Vw2 = 2u − V the work becomes 2ρAVu(V − u), maximised at u = V/2. This is exactly the Pelton-wheel result.'),
      P('How is the force on a pipe bend calculated?', 'Resultant of the momentum change ρQ(V2 − V1) and the pressure forces at the two sections.', 'Add forces vectorially.', 'For a bend turning through θ, Fx = ρQ(V1 − V2 cosθ) + p1A1 − p2A2 cosθ and Fy = ρQV2 sinθ + p2A2 sinθ in the usual notation. The support and anchor resist this resultant.'),
      P('Compute the force of a 60 mm jet at 15 m/s on a fixed vertical plate.', 'F = ρAV² = 1000 × 2.83 × 10⁻³ × 225 = 636.2 N.', 'F = ρAV².', 'Jet area A = π(0.06)²/4 = 2.83 × 10⁻³ m², so F = 1000 × 0.00283 × 15² = 636.2 N along the jet axis. The plate supplies this force to stop the jet.'),
      P('Define vena contracta and the coefficients Cc, Cv and Cd of an orifice.', 'Vena contracta is the narrowest jet section; Cc = a(c)/a, Cv = V(act)/V(th) and Cd = Cc × Cv.', 'Cd = Cc Cv.', 'For a sharp-edged orifice Cc ≈ 0.62–0.64, Cv ≈ 0.95–0.98 and Cd ≈ 0.60–0.62. Cc comes from the measured jet area and Cv from the jet trajectory and the head.'),
      P('Write the discharge through a sharp-edged orifice.', 'Q = Cd × a × √(2gH).', 'Q = Cd a √(2gH).', 'H is the head above the orifice centre. The coefficient Cd accounts for both the jet contraction and the velocity reduction caused by friction.'),
      P('What is a mouthpiece and what is its Cd?', 'A short tube attached to an orifice that modifies the discharge; an external cylindrical mouthpiece has Cd ≈ 0.855.', 'Tube length 2–3 diameters.', 'An external cylindrical mouthpiece running full gives Cd ≈ 0.855, higher than a sharp orifice because the tube suppresses part of the contraction loss. The value depends on whether it runs full or free.'),
      P('Give the discharge coefficient of a Borda (re-entrant) mouthpiece.', 'Cd ≈ 0.707 when running full and about 0.5 when running free.', 'Borda mouthpiece ≈ 0.707 full.', 'The re-entrant tube projects inside the tank and creates a fuller contraction, so it discharges less than the external mouthpiece. It is used in simple jet and calibrating devices.'),
      P('What is a convergent-divergent mouthpiece used for?', 'To obtain a discharge coefficient close to unity, Cd ≈ 1.0.', 'Pressure recovery in the divergent part.', 'The converging part accelerates the flow and the diverging part recovers pressure gradually, so losses are small and Cd approaches 1. Such mouthpieces suit accurate low-loss metering.'),
      P('Give the time to lower a tank of area A from head H1 to H2 through an orifice.', 't = 2A(√H1 − √H2)/(Cd a √(2g)).', 'Integrate dH with Q = Cd a √(2gH).', 'For complete emptying put H2 = 0, giving t = 2A√H1/(Cd a √(2g)). A is the tank area and a the orifice area.'),
      P('Write the discharge over a rectangular notch.', 'Q = (2/3)Cd b √(2g) H^(3/2) = 1.84 b H^(3/2) for Cd = 0.62.', 'Q ∝ H^(3/2).', 'b is the crest length and H the head over the sill. Integrating the element b√(2gH)dH from 0 to H gives the 3/2 power law.'),
      P('Write the discharge over a triangular (V) notch.', 'Q = (8/15)Cd tan(θ/2)√(2g) H^(5/2); for a 90° notch with Cd = 0.6, Q = 1.417 H^(5/2).', 'Q ∝ H^(5/2).', 'θ is the notch angle and H the head above the apex. The V-notch is accurate for small discharges because a large share of its perimeter is active at low flows.'),
      P('Write the discharge over a trapezoidal notch.', 'Q = (2/3)Cd1 b √(2g) H^(3/2) + (8/15)Cd2 tan(θ/2)√(2g) H^(5/2).', 'Rectangular part plus triangular parts.', 'The rectangular and triangular portions act as independent notches. When the sides are sized to cancel the end contractions, the section becomes a Cipolletti weir.'),
      P('What is a Cipolletti weir?', 'A trapezoidal weir with side slopes of 1 horizontal to 4 vertical; its discharge is Q = 1.859 b H^(3/2).', 'Side slope 1:4.', 'The added triangular areas compensate for the end contractions, so no end-contraction correction is needed. The constant 1.859 corresponds to Cd = 0.63.'),
      P('Write the discharge over a submerged (drowned) weir.', 'Q = (2/3)Cd1 √(2g) b (H1 − H2)^(3/2) + Cd2 b H2 √(2g(H1 − H2)).', 'Free part plus drowned part.', 'H1 is the upstream head and H2 the downstream head over the crest. The first term treats the head difference as a free weir and the second term accounts for the submerged flow.'),
      P('How are end contractions of a rectangular weir accounted for?', 'Effective crest length becomes L − 0.1nH, giving Q = 1.84(L − 0.1nH)H^(3/2).', '0.1H is lost per contraction.', 'Each of the n end contractions shortens the effective crest by about 0.1H. A full-width weir has no contractions, so n = 0.'),
      P('What is the velocity-of-approach correction in weirs?', 'Add Va²/2g to the measured head, so H becomes H + Va²/2g.', 'Va = Q/channel area.', 'The approaching stream carries kinetic energy that adds discharge over the crest. The correction matters for short weirs in narrow channels and is negligible when the approach velocity is small.'),
      P('Give typical discharge coefficients of common devices.', 'Orifice ≈ 0.62; venturimeter ≈ 0.98; rectangular weir ≈ 0.62; Cipolletti ≈ 0.63; mouthpiece ≈ 0.71–0.98.', 'Each device has its own Cd.', 'Sharp-crested weirs give Cd ≈ 0.60–0.65 while broad-crested weirs give 0.55–0.80. The coefficient depends on the Reynolds number, roughness and geometry, and is used at the actual head.'),
      P('Distinguish laminar and turbulent flow.', 'Laminar flow is orderly with parallel layers and no mixing; turbulent flow is chaotic with fluctuations and strong mixing.', 'Reynolds number decides the regime.', 'Laminar flow has a parabolic velocity profile and head loss proportional to velocity; turbulent flow has a flatter profile and head loss nearly proportional to velocity squared.'),
      P('Define the Reynolds number and its significance.', 'Re = ρVD/μ = VD/ν; the ratio of inertia to viscous forces.', 'Re = VD/ν.', 'For pipe flow, Re below 2000 indicates laminar and Re above 4000 turbulent flow. It also governs the similarity of flows and appears in pipe, boundary-layer and drag correlations.'),
      P('What is the critical Reynolds number range for pipe flow?', 'Transition from laminar to turbulent flow occurs roughly between Re = 2000 and Re = 4000.', 'Say 2000–4000.', 'Re = 2000 is the usual lower critical value below which flow stays laminar; turbulence is well developed above about 4000. Entrance disturbances and roughness lower the actual transition value.'),
      P('State the Hagen–Poiseuille law.', 'Q = π Δp d⁴/(128 μ L) for steady laminar flow in a straight circular pipe.', 'Q ∝ d⁴.', 'It assumes steady, fully developed, incompressible laminar flow. The corresponding head loss is hf = 32μVL/(ρgd²), and the velocity profile is parabolic with the mean velocity equal to half the maximum.'),
      P('Give the head loss and velocity relation for laminar pipe flow.', 'hf = 32μVL/(ρgd²), and the mean velocity equals half the centre-line maximum.', 'Parabolic profile, V(mean) = V(max)/2.', 'The parabolic profile u = (Δp/(4μL))(R² − r²) integrates to Q = πΔpR⁴/(8μL), which with d = 2R is exactly the Hagen–Poiseuille law.'),
      P('Write the Darcy–Weisbach equation.', 'hf = f L V²/(2 g d).', 'hf = fLV²/2gd.', 'f is the dimensionless friction factor. For laminar flow f = 64/Re; for turbulent flow f is read from the Moody chart or computed from the Colebrook equation.'),
      P('What is the friction factor for laminar pipe flow?', 'f = 64/Re.', 'Inversely proportional to Re.', 'Substituting f = 64/Re in the Darcy–Weisbach equation reproduces the Hagen–Poiseuille head loss, confirming that the two formulations are consistent.'),
      P('What does the Moody chart represent?', 'Friction factor f against Reynolds number with relative roughness ε/d as a parameter.', 'f versus Re, family of ε/d curves.', 'The laminar region is the line f = 64/Re. The turbulent region runs from the smooth-pipe curve to the fully rough zone where f is constant. The Colebrook equation is its analytical form.'),
      P('Write the Colebrook equation.', '1/√f = −2 log10[ε/(3.7d) + 2.51/(Re√f)].', 'Implicit; must be iterated.', 'It is valid for turbulent flow in commercial (rough) pipes and is the basis of the Moody chart. For a smooth pipe ε = 0 it reduces to the smooth-pipe relation.'),
      P('Write the Blasius friction formula for smooth turbulent pipe flow.', 'f = 0.316/Re^(1/4) for Re up to about 10⁵.', 'Simple power fit for smooth pipes.', 'The Blasius equation avoids iteration and is accurate for smooth pipes in the range about 4×10³ to 10⁵ in Reynolds number. It is a particular case of the smooth-pipe portion of the Moody chart.'),
      P('What is the entrance loss at a sharp pipe entry?', 'hL = 0.5 V²/2g.', 'k = 0.5.', 'The jet contracts to a vena contracta and re-expands inside the pipe, dissipating energy. A rounded bellmouth entry cuts k to about 0.05 while a re-entrant entry raises it to about 0.8.'),
      P('What is the exit loss into a reservoir?', 'hL = V²/2g, the whole velocity head is destroyed.', 'k = 1.0.', 'The fluid decelerates to the tank velocity, nearly zero, so all the kinetic energy is dissipated. The exit loss is usually the largest single minor loss in a discharge line.'),
      P('Write the loss at a sudden enlargement.', 'hL = (V1 − V2)²/2g.', 'Borda–Carnot loss.', 'The momentum balance across the abrupt expansion gives this turbulent-mixing loss. A gradual taper between the two sizes recovers most of that energy.'),
      P('Write the loss at a sudden contraction.', 'hL ≈ 0.5 V2²/2g for a sharp-edged contraction.', 'Vena-contracta loss.', 'Applying the Borda–Carnot expression between the vena contracta and the downstream pipe gives the 0.5 factor. A well-rounded contraction has a loss coefficient as low as 0.04.'),
      P('How are bend, elbow and valve losses expressed?', 'hL = k V²/2g; a 90° elbow has k ≈ 0.9, a globe valve 5–10 and a full-open gate valve about 0.2.', 'k from standard tables.', 'The loss coefficient depends on geometry and Reynolds number. Fitting losses dominate short plant piping while pipe friction dominates long mains.'),
      P('State the rules for pipes in series.', 'The discharge is the same in all pipes and the head losses add; L(eq)/d(eq)⁵ = Σ(Li/di⁵).', 'Q same, ΣhL.', 'For pipes joined end to end Q1 = Q2 = Q3 and the total head loss is the sum of the individual losses. The equivalent-length method replaces the assembly by one pipe of the chosen diameter.'),
      P('State the rules for pipes in parallel.', 'Each branch has the same head loss and the total discharge is the sum of the branch discharges.', 'hL same, Q adds.', 'Parallel branches share the same available head difference, so each may carry a different flow. They behave like resistors in parallel and reduce the total loss for a given total flow.'),
      P('Define an equivalent pipe.', 'A hypothetical straight pipe of one uniform diameter passing the same discharge with the same head loss as the actual network.', 'L(eq)/d(eq)⁵ = Σ(Li/di⁵).', 'The equivalent length is found by adding the L/d⁵ terms of the series pipes. The method simplifies network calculations once an equivalent diameter is chosen, preserving both Q and hf.'),
      P('What is water hammer and how does a surge tank help?', 'Sudden closure raises pressure by Δp = ρcV; a surge tank absorbs the surge.', 'Δp = ρ c V.', 'Water hammer converts flow momentum into pressure when a valve closes quickly: Δp = ρcV with c ≈ √(K/ρ), slightly lower in elastic pipes. The surge tank, an open chamber near a turbine or pump, lets the water mass oscillate and reduces the hammer.'),
      P('What is a boundary layer?', 'The thin layer next to a solid surface where viscosity creates velocity gradients and friction.', 'Prandtl boundary-layer theory.', 'Outside the layer the flow is treated as inviscid. The layer grows from the leading edge, turns turbulent after transition, and controls the skin friction and drag of the body.'),
      P('Define displacement thickness δ*.', 'δ* = ∫(1 − u/U)dy across the layer, the distance by which the boundary is effectively displaced by the retarded flow.', 'Mass deficit per unit width.', 'The integral runs from the wall into the free stream. The outer flow behaves as if the wall were moved outward by δ*. For the laminar flat-plate layer δ* ≈ 1.72x/√Re(x).'),
      P('Define momentum and energy thicknesses.', 'θ = ∫(u/U)(1 − u/U)dy and δe = ∫(u/U)(1 − (u/U)²)dy across the layer.', 'Momentum and kinetic-energy defects.', 'The momentum thickness gives the skin friction: τ0 = ρU² dθ/dx. For the laminar Blasius layer θ ≈ 0.664x/√Re(x), while δe measures the kinetic-energy defect of the layer.'),
      P('Give the laminar boundary-layer thickness and transition range on a flat plate.', 'δ = 5x/√Re(x); transition to turbulence occurs around Re(x) = 3×10⁵ to 5×10⁵.', 'δ = 5x/√Re(x).', 'Re(x) = Ux/ν is measured from the leading edge. Below transition the layer is laminar (Blasius solution); beyond it the turbulent layer is much thicker and exerts far more friction.'),
      P('Give the turbulent boundary-layer growth and the separation condition.', 'δ ≈ 0.37x/Re(x)^(1/5) on a smooth plate; separation begins where the wall shear becomes zero under an adverse pressure gradient.', 'Adverse gradient detaches the flow.', 'The turbulent layer is thicker and resists separation longer than a laminar layer. Separation and the wake are controlled by streamlining, wall suction or blowing, and by shaping to keep a favourable pressure gradient.'),
      P('What is the friction of flow past a flat plate?', 'Ff = 0.5ρU²A·Cf; the laminar average Cf = 1.328/√Re(L) and turbulent about 0.074/Re(L)^(1/5).', 'Cf from the plate Reynolds number.', 'For a laminar plate layer the local coefficient is Cf = 0.664/√Re(x); integrating gives the average 1.328/√Re(L). Turbulent values follow the 1/5-power law and are much larger.'),
      P('Write the drag equation and name the two drag components.', 'FD = 0.5 ρ V² A CD, consisting of pressure (form) drag plus skin-friction drag.', 'CD depends on shape and Re.', 'For blunt bodies the wake pressure drag dominates; for thin streamlined bodies friction dominates. At very low Reynolds number a sphere follows Stokes drag with CD = 24/Re.'),
      P('How does streamlining reduce drag?', 'It delays separation, shrinks the wake and almost eliminates pressure drag.', 'Teardrop and aerofoil shapes.', 'A streamlined body keeps the boundary layer attached, so almost all its drag becomes skin friction, which is kept small. This is why fuselages, hulls and cars are shaped in a teardrop manner.'),
      P('State Stokes law for a sphere.', 'The drag on a small sphere at very low Reynolds number is F = 6πμrV.', 'Creeping flow, Re ≪ 1.', 'Balancing drag against the effective weight (ρs − ρf)gV(sphere) gives the terminal velocity. Stokes law is the basis of falling-sphere viscometry and particle-settling analysis.'),
      P('What is the terminal velocity of a falling sphere?', 'Vt = d²(ρs − ρf)g/(18μ).', 'Vt ∝ d².', 'It is reached when drag plus buoyancy balances the weight. For larger particles and higher Reynolds numbers the Stokes result must be corrected with a proper CD because Re is no longer small.'),
      P('What are the sphere drag-coefficient ranges?', 'CD = 24/Re for Re < 1, about 0.44 for Re near 10³–10⁵, then a sudden drop at the drag-crisis Reynolds number.', 'CD–Re chart of a sphere.', 'The laminar layer on a sphere separates near 80° from the stagnation point. When the layer becomes turbulent, separation moves rearward, the wake shrinks and CD falls sharply near Re ≈ 3×10⁵.'),
      P('How is lift generated and expressed?', 'By the pressure difference around the body: FL = 0.5 ρ V² A CL, and equivalently L = ρVΓ per unit span.', 'Kutta–Joukowski theorem.', 'An angled or cambered aerofoil deflects the flow and sets up circulation, producing lift at the price of induced drag. When the flow separates (stall), the lift and drag performance collapse.'),
      P('How is open-channel flow classified by depth variation?', 'Uniform, gradually varied and rapidly varied flow.', 'GVF and RVF regimes.', 'Uniform flow has constant depth and velocity (bed slope equals friction slope at the normal depth). Gradually varied flow changes depth slowly (backwater and drawdown curves); rapidly varied flow includes hydraulic jumps and crest flow.'),
      P('Write the Chezy equation and its units.', 'V = C√(RS); C has units of m^(1/2)/s and R the hydraulic radius in m.', 'V = C√(RS).', 'R = A/P is the hydraulic radius and S the bed or friction slope. The Chezy coefficient C is dimensional and relates to the Darcy friction factor by C = √(8g/f).'),
      P('Write the Manning equation and state the unit of n.', 'V = (1/n) R^(2/3) S^(1/2); n has units of s/m^(1/3).', 'n is the roughness coefficient.', 'Q = (1/n) A R^(2/3) S^(1/2). Typical n values are 0.012–0.015 for concrete, 0.013 for clean earth and 0.03–0.05 for natural vegetated streams.'),
      P('What is the most economical channel section?', 'The section giving the maximum discharge for a given area, i.e., the minimum wetted perimeter.', 'Optimum hydraulic radius R = d/2.', 'For a rectangle the best section has depth equal to half the width (B = 2d); for a trapezoid it is half a regular hexagon with side slopes at 60° to the horizontal. In both cases the hydraulic radius R = d/2.'),
      P('Where do maximum velocity and discharge occur in a circular channel?', 'Maximum velocity at a depth of about 0.81d and maximum discharge at about 0.95d.', 'Half-full gives R = d/4.', 'As the channel approaches full, the wetted perimeter grows faster than the area, so neither velocity nor discharge peaks at the brim-full condition. The results follow by maximising those functions on the circular section.'),
      P('Explain a hydraulic jump and its conjugate-depth relation.', 'A rapidly varied flow converting supercritical to subcritical; y2/y1 = 0.5(√(1 + 8Fr1²) − 1).', 'Forms when Fr1 > 1.', 'The jump dissipates a large amount of kinetic energy, so it is the standard energy dissipator below spillways and gates. It is also used for mixing chemicals and raising downstream levels.'),
      P('Define specific energy and critical depth.', 'E = y + V²/2g; for unit-width flow yc = (q²/g)^(1/3) and E(min) = 1.5 yc.', 'E = y + q²/(2gy²).', 'Specific energy is the depth head plus velocity head measured from the bed. At the critical depth the Froude number is unity and E is minimum; for any larger E there are two alternate depths, one subcritical and one supercritical.'),
      P('How is open-channel flow classified by the Froude number?', 'Fr < 1 subcritical (tranquil), Fr = 1 critical, Fr > 1 supercritical (rapid) flow.', 'Fr = V/√(gy).', 'Fr = V/√(gL), with L the hydraulic depth A/T. Subcritical flow is controlled from downstream and supercritical from upstream; critical flow corresponds to the minimum specific energy.'),
      P('State the principle of dimensional homogeneity.', 'Every term of a correct physical equation must have the same dimensions.', 'Fourier principle.', 'It is used to check formulas and to discover the structure of physical laws. For example all the terms of the Bernoulli equation are lengths, and all the terms of an energy balance are joules.'),
      P('What is the Rayleigh method of dimensional analysis?', 'The dependent variable is written as a power product of the independent variables, and the exponents are found by matching dimensions.', 'V = k D^a ρ^b μ^c and so on.', 'The unknown exponents follow from the requirement of equal dimensions on both sides. The method is simple but needs the correct set of relevant variables to be listed first.'),
      P('State the Buckingham π theorem.', 'If a problem involves n variables and m fundamental dimensions, it can be expressed in n − m independent dimensionless π groups.', 'π groups = n − m.', 'With mass, length and time as the fundamental dimensions, m = 3. The repeating variables must be chosen so that none is dimensionless, none is a combination of the others, and together they contain all three base dimensions.'),
      P('Give the physical significance of the Reynolds number.', 'It is the ratio of inertia to viscous forces and the criterion for laminar or turbulent flow.', 'Re = ρVD/μ.', 'The Reynolds number governs pipe regimes, boundary-layer behaviour and drag. Dynamic similarity of such flows requires equal Reynolds numbers in the model and the prototype.'),
      P('Give the physical meanings of the Froude, Mach, Weber and Euler numbers.', 'Fr = V/√(gL) (inertia/gravity); Ma = V/c (inertia/elasticity); We = ρV²L/σ (inertia/surface tension); Eu = Δp/(ρV²) (pressure/inertia).', 'One number per dominating force pair.', 'Fr classifies open-channel flow and waves; Ma separates subsonic from supersonic flow with shocks at Ma = 1; We matters in droplets and sprays; Eu is used for pumps, valves and cavitation studies.'),
      P('Name the three types of similitude between model and prototype.', 'Geometric, kinematic and dynamic similarity.', 'Shape, motion, then forces.', 'Geometric similarity keeps the length scale constant; kinematic similarity keeps the ratios of velocities and accelerations; dynamic similarity keeps the ratios of the governing forces. Full similarity requires all three together.'),
      P('When are the Reynolds and Froude model laws applied, and why are distorted models used?', 'Reynolds law for viscous-dominated submerged flows, Froude law for free-surface flows; distorted models use unequal horizontal and vertical scales where the prototype is too shallow.', 'Equal Re or equal Fr in model and prototype.', 'Distorted models exaggerate the vertical scale so that depths and pressures are measurable in rivers and harbours, sacrificing geometric similarity and correcting the results empirically with slope-based Chezy or Manning relations.'),
    ];
SSC_JE_ENC_FLUIDS.hydmach = [
      P('On what basis are hydraulic turbines classified?', 'By their action (impulse or reaction) and by the direction of flow through the runner.', 'Action and flow direction.', 'Impulse turbines convert pressure into kinetic energy in a nozzle; reaction turbines work with a pressure change through the runner. The flow may be tangential, radial, axial or mixed.'),
      P('Into what classes are turbines grouped by flow direction?', 'Tangential, radial, axial and mixed-flow turbines.', 'Pelton, Francis, Kaplan.', 'The Pelton wheel is a tangential-flow machine, the Francis turbine is an inward radial-flow machine, and Kaplan or propeller turbines are axial-flow machines. Mixed-flow runners combine radial and axial motion across the blades.'),
      P('Define an impulse turbine.', 'A turbine in which the whole pressure head is first converted into kinetic energy in the nozzle, and the free jet strikes the vanes at atmospheric pressure.', 'No pressure change across the wheel.', 'The nozzle produces a high-speed jet at atmospheric pressure; the runner merely extracts the jet momentum. No pressure casing or draft tube is required, and the jet travels partly in air between the nozzle and the buckets.'),
      P('Define a reaction turbine.', 'A turbine whose runner is completely submerged and works with the pressure of the water changing as it passes through the moving vanes.', 'Pressure change inside the runner.', 'The water fills the runner passages, and only part of the head is converted into kinetic energy before entry. Such turbines are enclosed in a pressure casing and fitted with a draft tube.'),
      P('Which turbine type needs a full-pressure casing?', 'The reaction turbine.', 'Pelton needs none.', 'Because the water passing through the runner of a reaction turbine is under pressure above atmospheric, a strong watertight spiral casing is required. An impulse wheel runs at atmospheric pressure and needs only a guard casing.'),
      P('Give typical examples of each turbine class.', 'Pelton: tangential impulse; Francis: radial inward reaction; Kaplan and propeller: axial reaction.', 'One example per class.', 'The Pelton wheel suits very high heads, the Francis turbine medium heads with radial inward flow, and the Kaplan or propeller machines low heads with axial flow. This classification guides the specific-speed selection.'),
      P('How is a turbine selected according to head?', 'Very high heads use Pelton, medium heads use Francis, and low heads use Kaplan or propeller runners.', 'Head governs the type.', 'Rough ranges: Pelton above about 250 m with small discharge, Francis about 60–250 m, and Kaplan below about 60 m with large discharge. The governing factor is the combination of head and discharge required.'),
      P('What is governing of a turbine?', 'The automatic regulation of water admission to keep the runner speed constant as the electrical load changes.', 'Flow control to hold N constant.', 'When the load falls, the governing mechanism reduces the discharge (spear in Pelton, guide vanes in reaction machines) so that the torque output matches the smaller load.'),
      P('What is a Pelton wheel?', 'A tangential-flow impulse turbine used for very high heads and low discharges.', 'High head, low flow.', 'A high-speed jet from a nozzle strikes the double-cup buckets at the rim and gives up its momentum. It runs at atmospheric pressure, needs no draft tube and gives a high efficiency under a constant head.'),
      P('Name the main parts of a Pelton wheel.', 'Nozzle with spear, runner with buckets, shaft, casing, brake nozzle and the governor.', 'Six main components.', 'The penstock supplies the nozzle, the spear regulates the jet area, the buckets receive the jet, and the brake jet stops the runner quickly during emergency shutdown.'),
      P('What is the function of the spear in a Pelton wheel?', 'It moves axially inside the nozzle to vary the jet cross-section and hence the discharge.', 'Regulates flow for governing.', 'Advancing the spear narrows the nozzle orifice and cuts the discharge while retracting it increases the flow. This smooth regulation keeps the speed constant as the load changes.'),
      P('Describe the bucket construction of a Pelton runner.', 'Each bucket is a double hemispherical cup with a sharp central splitter that divides the jet into two symmetrical parts.', 'Splitter ridge divides the jet.', 'The splitter faces the incoming jet and halves it, and the cup deflects each half through about 165°, reversing the relative velocity almost completely. The symmetry balances the side thrust on the bearings.'),
      P('What is the velocity of the jet entering a Pelton bucket?', 'V1 = Cv√(2gH), with the head H and the nozzle coefficient Cv ≈ 0.97–0.99.', 'V1 = Cv√(2gH).', 'The nozzle friction reduces the theoretical √(2gH). With Cv = 0.98 and H = 50 m, V1 = 0.98 × √981 = 0.98 × 31.32 = 30.7 m/s.'),
      P('Define the speed ratio φ of a Pelton wheel.', 'φ = u/√(2gH), with u = πDN/60 the bucket speed; practical values are 0.43–0.47.', 'u = πDN/60.', 'The speed ratio links the peripheral speed of the buckets to the head. Around the optimum, φ ≈ 0.46 keeps the relative jet and the momentum transfer most effective.'),
      P('State the condition for maximum hydraulic efficiency of a Pelton wheel.', 'The bucket speed must be half the jet velocity: u = V1/2.', 'u = V1/2.', 'For ideal buckets the efficiency is ηh = 4u(V1 − u)/V1², and differentiating shows a maximum at u = V1/2 where the theoretical efficiency reaches 100 per cent. Real wheels reach about 90 per cent near φ ≈ 0.46.'),
      P('What is the work done per kilogram of water in a Pelton wheel?', 'W = u(Vw1 − Vw2) = 2u(V1 − u) for ideal buckets.', 'W = u(Vw1 − Vw2).', 'With Vw1 = V1 and, for a fully reversing bucket, Vw2 = 2u − V1, the work becomes 2u(V1 − u) J/kg. This momentum-change expression is the Euler turbine equation applied to the wheel.'),
      P('Write the hydraulic efficiency of a Pelton wheel.', 'ηh = (Vw1 − Vw2)u/(gH) = 2u(V1 − u)/(gH); with V1 = √(2gH) this becomes 4u(V1 − u)/V1².', 'ηh = work/gH.', 'At u = V1/2 the ideal efficiency is 100 per cent. In practice, bucket friction, jet interference and losses reduce it to about 85–90 per cent at the design point.'),
      P('Why does a Pelton wheel not need a casing or draft tube?', 'The water leaves the buckets into the atmosphere at the same pressure, so no pressurised housing is required.', 'Impulse action at atmospheric pressure.', 'Since no pressure change occurs at the runner, a pressure casing is unnecessary; the casing only prevents splashing and protects personnel. A draft tube is also not needed because there is no exit pressure head to recover.'),
      P('What is the jet ratio of a Pelton wheel and its usual value?', 'm = D/d, the ratio of pitch diameter to jet diameter; usually 10–20, with about 12 giving the best efficiency.', 'm = D/d.', 'Both too-tight and too-loose spacing hurt the efficiency: closely spaced buckets choke the jet while widely spaced ones waste parts of it. The optimum jet ratio is usually chosen near m ≈ 12.'),
      P('How is the number of buckets on a Pelton runner found?', 'z = 15 + D/(2d), where D is the pitch diameter and d the jet diameter.', 'z = 15 + D/2d.', 'The empirical formula ensures that a bucket is always in the jet when the previous one leaves it, so no part of the jet is lost. It gives about 15–50 buckets for practical wheel sizes.'),
      P('Why are Pelton wheels sometimes given several jets?', 'To handle a larger discharge when the jet ratio limits the single-jet diameter.', 'Up to six nozzles per wheel.', 'One jet is limited by the jet ratio and bucket size, so for higher power the wheel is fed by several nozzles placed around the rim, each with its own spear. This raises the capacity without changing the head.'),
      P('What is the purpose of the braking jet on a Pelton wheel?', 'It directs a reverse jet onto the backs of the buckets to bring the runner to rest quickly when the main jet is shut off.', 'Emergency stopper.', 'After the main nozzle closes, the runner still spins for a long time; the small brake jet opposes the motion and stops the wheel in a fraction of the time, protecting the machine during emergencies and maintenance.'),
      P('How is bucket friction represented in the Pelton velocity triangle?', 'By the coefficient k: Vr2 = k·Vr1, where k ≈ 0.8–0.98.', 'Vr2 = k(V1 − u).', 'The relative velocity leaving the bucket is less than the entering relative velocity V1 − u because of surface friction and turbulence. A smaller k lowers the work done and the hydraulic efficiency.'),
      P('Describe the Pelton velocity triangle at inlet and outlet.', 'Inlet: jet velocity V1 with bucket speed u, giving relative velocity Vr1 = V1 − u; outlet: Vr2 turned through nearly 180°.', 'Nearly reversed relative flow.', 'At the inlet the flow is tangential, so Vw1 = V1. At the outlet the bucket deflects the relative stream backward, giving Vw2 ≈ 2u − V1 for the ideal case, which maximises the momentum exchange.'),
      P('Compute the jet velocity for a Pelton wheel working under a 50 m head with Cv = 0.98.', 'V1 = 0.98 × √(2 × 9.81 × 50) = 0.98 × 31.32 = 30.7 m/s.', '√(2 × 9.81 × 50) = 31.32 m/s.', 'V1 = Cv√(2gH) = 0.98 × √981 = 0.98 × 31.32 = 30.69 ≈ 30.7 m/s. The jet diameter then follows from the discharge and this velocity.'),
      P('What bucket speed gives the maximum efficiency when the jet velocity V1 = 40 m/s?', 'u = V1/2 = 20 m/s.', 'u = V1/2.', 'The optimum bucket velocity is half the jet velocity. With u = 20 m/s the ideal work per kilogram is 2u(V1 − u) = 2 × 20 × 20 = 800 J/kg, which is the maximum for that jet.'),
      P('Find the speed of a Pelton wheel with a 1 m pitch diameter at the optimum condition for a 50 m head (φ = 0.46).', 'u = φ√(2gH) = 0.46 × 31.32 = 14.4 m/s; N = 60u/(πD) = 275 rpm.', 'u = φ√(2gH), N = 60u/πD.', 'u = 0.46 × 31.32 = 14.41 m/s, so N = 60 × 14.41/(π × 1) = 275 rpm. The speed ratio thus fixes the wheel speed for a chosen head and diameter.'),
      P('Compute the shaft power of a Pelton wheel with Q = 0.05 m³/s and a specific work of 2000 J/kg.', 'P = ρQ·W = 1000 × 0.05 × 2000 = 100,000 W = 100 kW.', 'P = ρQ(Vw1 − Vw2)u.', 'The runner power is the mass flow rate times the specific work. Ignoring bearing friction this equals the shaft power: 1000 × 0.05 × 2000 = 100 kW.'),
      P('What is the effect of the bucket outlet angle on Pelton performance?', 'A smaller outlet angle (about 15°) gives a more complete reversal; the work carries the factor (1 + k cosφ).', 'cos φ ≈ 0.97 for 15°.', 'Real buckets deflect the jet by about 165°, leaving an outlet angle φ ≈ 15° with the reverse direction. Imperfect reversal costs energy, so the turn-around factor (1 + k cosφ) appears in the work formula.'),
      P('What head and discharge range suits a Pelton wheel?', 'Very high heads (above about 250 m) with small to medium discharge.', 'High-head, low-flow machine.', 'Pelton wheels are chosen where the head is large and the discharge comparatively small, which keeps the specific speed low (8.5–30). Multiple jets can raise the power capacity at the same head.'),
      P('What overall efficiency is typical for a large Pelton wheel?', 'About 85–90 per cent at the design point.', 'ηo ≈ 0.85–0.90.', 'The bulk of the loss is hydraulic (nozzle, bucket friction and jet interference) with small mechanical losses in the bearings. Modern, well-designed Pelton installations exceed 90 per cent.'),
      P('What is a Francis turbine?', 'An inward radial-flow reaction turbine suited to medium heads.', 'Medium-head reaction machine.', 'Water enters the spiral casing, passes through the guide vanes into the runner radially inward, and leaves axially through the draft tube. Its speed and discharge lie between the Pelton and Kaplan families.'),
      P('Name the main parts of a Francis turbine.', 'Spiral casing, stay ring, guide vanes (wicket gates), runner, shaft and draft tube.', 'Six components.', 'The casing distributes water uniformly around the periphery, the guide vanes control the flow and swirl, the runner converts the energy, and the draft tube recovers the exit kinetic head.'),
      P('What is the function of the spiral casing?', 'To distribute the water uniformly and without shock around the whole runner circumference, with a progressively decreasing area.', 'Uniform admission.', 'The cross-section shrinks along the circumference so that a similar discharge is delivered at every point around the runner. This uniform admission avoids swirl and keeps the runner balanced.'),
      P('What is the function of the guide vanes in a Francis turbine?', 'To control the amount of water admitted and to direct it onto the runner at the correct angle.', 'Governing and guidance.', 'Rotating the annular row of adjustable vanes changes the throat passage and hence the flow as the load changes, while their curvature sets the inlet whirl velocity Vw1 required by the runner.'),
      P('Are the Francis runner blades adjustable?', 'No; the runner blades are fixed, and flow is regulated only by the guide vanes.', 'Fixed runner blades.', 'The blades are cast or welded onto the hub and shroud, so part-load adjustment cannot happen at the runner. All governing is done through the wicket gates, and the turbine runs at nearly constant speed.'),
      P('What is the function of the draft tube in a Francis turbine?', 'To convert the residual kinetic energy of the leaving water into pressure and to allow the runner to be set above the tail race.', 'Recover exit kinetic head.', 'The gradually expanding duct slows the water from the runner velocity to the tail-race velocity and converts the difference V²/2g into pressure. It also creates a suction that lets the runner stand above the tail level.'),
      P('Write the work done per kilogram in a Francis turbine.', 'W = (Vw1u1 − Vw2u2) J/kg, where u1 = πD1N/60.', 'Euler turbine equation.', 'The work per unit mass equals the change of moment of momentum, Vw1u1 − Vw2u2, with u1 and u2 the peripheral speeds at the inlet and outlet radii. For an axial discharge with Vw2 = 0 the second term vanishes.'),
      P('What is the condition for maximum efficiency in a Francis turbine?', 'The leaving water should have zero whirl, Vw2 = 0, discharging radially or axially without swirl.', 'Vw2 = 0.', 'All the whirl momentum is then extracted by the runner and no energy is wasted in the tangential swirling of the draft-tube flow. This radial-discharge condition is the design target at the rated head.'),
      P('Write the hydraulic efficiency of a Francis turbine.', 'ηh = (Vw1u1 − Vw2u2)/(gH).', 'Runner work per unit weight over head.', 'It compares the theoretical work from the Euler equation with the available head H. Real machines reach hydraulic efficiencies of about 0.90–0.95 at the design head and stay high over a broad load range.'),
      P('List the losses in a Francis turbine.', 'Hydraulic losses (runner and draft-tube friction and shock), leakage past the runner, disc friction and bearing losses.', 'Four groups of losses.', 'The mechanical losses lower the shaft output relative to the runner power, so the overall efficiency is the product of the hydraulic, volumetric and mechanical efficiencies of the machine.'),
      P('What head and discharge conditions suit a Francis turbine?', 'Medium heads of roughly 60–250 m with moderate discharges.', 'Between Pelton and Kaplan.', 'The Francis wheel occupies the middle of the specific-speed range (60–300), avoiding the great size of a Pelton plant and the high-speed runner of a Kaplan machine for the given duty.'),
      P('Compute the peripheral speed u1 at a Francis runner inlet with φ = 0.7 and H = 30 m.', 'u1 = φ√(2gH) = 0.7 × 24.26 = 16.98 ≈ 17 m/s.', 'u1 = φ√(2gH).', '√(2 × 9.81 × 30) = √588.6 = 24.26 m/s, so u1 = 0.7 × 24.26 = 16.98 m/s. The runner diameter and speed then follow from u1 = πD1N/60.'),
      P('How does the draft tube contribute to the reaction effect of a Francis turbine?', 'By keeping the pressure below atmospheric at the runner exit, it lets the machine use the suction head and recover part of the exit kinetic energy.', 'Reaction comes from pressure recovery.', 'The draft tube brings the exit pressure toward the tail-race pressure through gradual expansion, so the runner senses a suction below the tail level. This adds the draft-tube head to the working head of the machine.'),
      P('How does the stay ring differ from the guide vanes?', 'The stay ring is a fixed structural ring that carries the casing loads, while the guide vanes are the movable regulating elements just ahead of the runner.', 'Fixed ring versus movable vanes.', 'The stay ring transmits the pressure loads of the casing onto the concrete, while the wicket-gate vanes rotate to control the flow. Both rings together direct the water smoothly onto the runner.'),
      P('What is a Kaplan turbine?', 'An axial-flow reaction turbine with adjustable runner blades, used for low heads and large discharges.', 'Adjustable axial runner.', 'Its blade pitch can be changed while running, which keeps the efficiency high over a wide range of load and head. It has the highest specific speed of the common hydraulic turbines.'),
      P('How does a propeller turbine differ from a Kaplan turbine?', 'The propeller turbine has fixed runner blades while the Kaplan runner blades are adjustable.', 'Adjustability is the difference.', 'Otherwise the two are mechanically alike, being axial-flow machines for low heads. The Kaplan can follow the optimum blade angle as conditions change, while the propeller turbine is simpler but has a narrower high-efficiency range.'),
      P('What is the advantage of the adjustable Kaplan blades?', 'The blade angle can be matched to the instantaneous load and head, keeping the efficiency high over a wide operating range.', 'Blade setting follows the wicket gates.', 'When the load changes, the pitch is varied together with the guide-vane opening. This maintains a near-uniform swirl and incidence at the runner and avoids the sharp efficiency drop of the fixed-blade machines at part load.'),
      P('What head range suits a Kaplan or propeller turbine?', 'Low heads of roughly 2–70 m with very large discharges.', 'Low head, high discharge.', 'Axial runners pass a large flow area with a small diameter, so they are ideal for run-of-river and high-discharge sites. Their specific speed measures 300–1000 (MKS units).'),
      P('Describe the runner and shaft layout of a Kaplan turbine.', 'A few blades (usually 3–8) mounted on a large hub on a vertical shaft, enclosed by the spiral casing and the draft tube.', 'Vertical-shaft machine.', 'The blades resemble ship propellers and can be turned about their radial axes. The vertical arrangement keeps the generator above and gives a short, efficient draft-tube path to the tail race.'),
      P('What is the tangential-velocity characteristic of axial-flow turbines?', 'The peripheral speed u is the same at inlet and outlet because the radius is constant.', 'u1 = u2 = u.', 'For purely axial flow, u2 = u1 = u, so the Euler equation reduces to work per kg = u(Vw1 − Vw2). This simplifies the velocity-triangle design on cylindrical blade sections.'),
      P('How is the discharge through a Kaplan runner obtained?', 'Q = (π/4)(D² − d²)·Vf, using the annulus between the tip diameter D and the hub diameter d.', 'Annular flow area.', 'The axial velocity Vf is nearly constant over the annulus, so the flow equals the annulus area times Vf. The large annulus area is why a Kaplan runner passes such a big discharge at low head.'),
      P('How does the part-load efficiency of a Kaplan turbine compare with a fixed-blade machine?', 'The adjustable Kaplan keeps a flat efficiency curve, while the propeller efficiency falls steeply at part load.', 'Adjustable pitch wins at part load.', 'By pitching the blades together with the guide vanes, the Kaplan sustains high efficiency from about 25 per cent to full load. This makes it the preferred machine when the load or head varies considerably.'),
      P('Why must a Kaplan turbine be set deeper relative to the tail race?', 'Because its high specific speed needs a large critical Thoma factor, so a deeper setting avoids cavitation.', 'High Ns means a deep setting.', 'The high-speed, low-head runner operates at very low local pressures near the blades, so it demands a large pressure margin. Deeper submergence raises the local head and keeps the pressure above the vapour pressure.'),
      P('Write the Euler turbine equation.', 'Work done per kg of fluid = (Vw1u1 − Vw2u2).', 'Moment of momentum change.', 'From the angular-momentum theorem, torque = ρQ(r1Vw1 − r2Vw2); multiplying by ω gives work per kg = ω(r1Vw1 − r2Vw2) = Vw1u1 − Vw2u2. For a pump the sign reverses because work is done on the fluid.'),
      P('What head does the Euler turbine work correspond to?', 'H(e) = (Vw1u1 − Vw2u2)/g.', 'Divide the specific work by g.', 'Dividing the specific work by g converts it into metres of head. This is the theoretical head utilised and is the numerator of the hydraulic efficiency of a reaction turbine.'),
      P('Simplify the Euler equation for an axial-flow machine.', 'Work per kg = u(Vw1 − Vw2), because u1 = u2 = u.', 'Axial: constant radius.', 'In Kaplan and propeller turbines the blade radius is identical at inlet and outlet, so the peripheral speeds are equal. The equation then depends only on the change of whirl velocity across the runner.'),
      P('Define hydraulic efficiency of a turbine.', 'ηh = (Vw1u1 − Vw2u2)/(gH), the runner work divided by the available water power.', 'Euler work over available head energy.', 'Hydraulic efficiency excludes bearing friction and leakage. For an impulse wheel with no draft tube the residual whirl is lost, while a reaction machine recovers part of it in the draft tube.'),
      P('Define mechanical efficiency of a turbine.', 'ηm = shaft power/runner power, the fraction of runner power that reaches the shaft.', 'Bearing and windage losses.', 'Runner power is ρQ(Vw1u1 − Vw2u2). The mechanical efficiency lies typically between 0.95 and 0.98 because journal and thrust bearing friction and disc windage dissipate a little runner power.'),
      P('Define volumetric efficiency of a turbine.', 'ηv = (Q − q)/Q, where q is the leakage past the runner clearance.', 'Leakage loss.', 'Some water escapes through the gap between the runner and the casing without doing work. Volumetric efficiency compares the useful flow with the total flow entering the machine.'),
      P('Define the overall efficiency of a turbine and relate it to the others.', 'ηo = shaft power/water power = ηh × ηm × ηv.', 'Product of three components.', 'Water power is ρgQH. Overall efficiency is the ratio of the useful shaft power to the hydraulic power supplied by the head and discharge, and equals the product of the hydraulic, mechanical and volumetric efficiencies.'),
      P('Compute the water power for Q = 10 m³/s under a head of 10 m.', 'Pw = ρgQH = 1000 × 9.81 × 10 × 10 = 981,000 W = 981 kW.', 'Pw = ρgQH.', 'Pw = 1000 × 9.81 × 10 × 10 = 981,000 W = 981 kW. This is the total hydraulic power available at the entrance to the machine.'),
      P('Compute the runner power for Q = 5 m³/s when Vw1u1 − Vw2u2 = 500 J/kg.', 'Pr = ρQ(Vw1u1 − Vw2u2) = 1000 × 5 × 500 = 2.5 × 10⁶ W = 2.5 MW.', 'Pr = ρQ × Euler work.', 'The runner power is the mass flow times the specific Euler work: 1000 × 5 × 500 = 2,500,000 W = 2.5 MW. Multiplying by the mechanical efficiency gives the shaft power.'),
      P('What is the shaft power if the water power is 981 kW and the overall efficiency is 0.9?', 'P(shaft) = ηo × Pw = 0.9 × 981 = 882.9 kW.', 'ηo = P(shaft)/P(water).', 'P(shaft) = 0.9 × 981 = 882.9 kW. The difference of 98.1 kW represents the hydraulic, volumetric and mechanical losses of the machine.'),
      P('What is meant by the degree of reaction of a turbine?', 'The fraction of the total head converted into pressure energy inside the runner itself.', 'Zero for impulse, near one for pure reaction.', 'An impulse wheel has zero degree of reaction because all the head becomes kinetic in the nozzle. A reaction runner converts a large part of the remaining head into pressure as the water passes through the blades, and the draft tube adds further recovery.'),
      P('Define the specific speed of a hydraulic turbine.', 'The speed of a geometrically similar turbine that would produce unit power under unit head.', 'Ns = N√P/H^(5/4).', 'It characterises the runner type and shape: with N in rpm, P in kW and H in m, Ns = N√P/H^(5/4). It is numerically dimensionless only in MKS engineering units and is the basis of runner selection.'),
      P('Write the specific-speed formula of a turbine in MKS units.', 'Ns = N√P/H^(5/4), with P in kW, H in m and N in rpm.', 'Ns = N√P/H^1.25.', 'The definition uses the shaft or rated power in kilowatts. The higher the specific speed, the faster and smaller the runner for a given head, and the lower the head that suits that machine type.'),
      P('Give the specific-speed ranges of Pelton, Francis and Kaplan turbines.', 'Pelton about 8.5–30, Francis about 60–300, Kaplan or propeller about 300–1000 (MKS, P in kW).', 'Three typical bands.', 'These MKS values follow from Ns = N√P/H^(5/4). Low specific speeds mean high heads with impulse action; the highest specific speeds belong to the axial machines on low heads.'),
      P('For which head is a low specific speed used?', 'Very high heads use low specific speeds, i.e., Pelton wheels.', 'Low Ns = high head.', 'At a high head a Pelton wheel runs slowly with a moderately large diameter, giving Ns as low as 8.5–30. Raising the head further calls for an even smaller Ns, hence fewer or smaller jets for the same power.'),
      P('How does the specific speed choose the runner shape?', 'Low Ns needs a deep slow Pelton-type runner, medium Ns a radial Francis runner, and high Ns a squat axial Kaplan runner.', 'Ns fixes the profile and speed.', 'The specific speed summarises the geometric proportion of the runner: deep buckets for impulse, narrow radial passages for Francis, and wide axial annuli for Kaplan. It therefore governs the layout of the whole machine.'),
      P('What is the unit speed of a hydraulic turbine?', 'The speed of a geometrically similar turbine working under a unit head of 1 m: Nu = N/√H.', 'Nu = N/√H.', 'For homologous runners the peripheral speed varies as the square root of the head, so the speed scales with √H. Along with unit discharge and unit power, it converts test data at one head into the performance at any other head.'),
      P('Define the unit discharge of a turbine.', 'The discharge passed by a geometrically similar turbine working under a unit head: Qu = Q/√H.', 'Qu = Q/√H.', 'The through-flow velocity varies as √(2gH), so at a fixed runner size the discharge scales with √H. At varying size the invariant is Q/(D²√H).'),
      P('What is the unit power of a turbine?', 'The power developed under a unit head: Pu = P/H^(3/2).', 'Pu = P/H^1.5.', 'Since the power equals ρgQH and the discharge scales as √H, the output scales as H^(3/2). The three unit quantities together describe the machine fully at any head from a single test.'),
      P('Why are unit quantities used in turbine testing?', 'To predict the performance of a turbine at any head from measurements taken at a single convenient test head.', 'Similarity scaling across heads.', 'A model or prototype is tested at one head and the records are retranscribed to the site head by scaling the speed by √H and the power by H^(3/2). This is the practical basis of characteristic-curve generation.'),
      P('What are constant-head characteristic curves of a turbine?', 'Plots of discharge Q, power P and efficiency η against the speed N while the head is held fixed.', 'Q, P and η versus N at constant H.', 'The runner is braked and run over a wide speed range under a fixed head to trace each curve. The power and efficiency peak near the design speed, and the curves also expose the run-away speed at zero output.'),
      P('What are constant-speed characteristic curves of a turbine?', 'Plots of discharge Q, power P and efficiency η against the head H while the speed is held fixed.', 'Q, P and η versus H at constant N.', 'A generator locks the turbine speed, and the head changes with the reservoir level and the load. These curves map the operating range at the rated speed and show where the efficiency stays acceptable.'),
      P('What are iso-efficiency or muschel curves of a turbine?', 'Contour lines joining points of equal efficiency drawn on the speed–discharge plane.', 'Equal-efficiency contours over N and Q.', 'They come from a grid of constant-head tests run at many speeds and heads. The closed contour family resembles a mussel shell, hence the name, and reveals the best operating region of the machine at a glance.'),
      P('What is the run-away speed of a turbine?', 'The highest speed the runner attains when the load is suddenly removed while the full discharge is still admitted.', 'Zero-load speed ceiling.', 'With no resisting torque the runner accelerates until its hydraulic losses balance the water power, typically 1.8 to 2.4 times the normal speed. The value fixes the rotor strength and the generator overspeed protection.'),
      P('Name the common types of draft tubes.', 'The straight diverging (conical) tube, the simple elbow tube, and the elbow with a long tapering exit called the Moody spreading tube.', 'Conical, elbow and Moody.', 'The straight tube recovers the most energy but needs a deep tail pit. Elbow tubes turn the discharge horizontal within a shallow pit and suit Kaplan machines, while the Moody type spreads the jet with a central splitter for better recovery.'),
      P('Write the efficiency of a draft tube.', 'η = 1 − (V3/V2)², the recovered head divided by the kinetic head entering the tube.', 'η = 1 − (V3/V2)².', 'The diverging passage slows the water from the runner-exit velocity V2 to the tail-race velocity V3 and converts (V2² − V3²)/(2g) into pressure. A slow even taper with a small exit velocity gives the best recovery.'),
      P('What causes cavitation in a reaction turbine?', 'The local pressure on the runner blades falls below the vapour pressure of the water, so vapour bubbles form and collapse violently in the higher-pressure region downstream.', 'Local pressure below vapour pressure.', 'High blade speeds at low head create very low pressures near the runner outlet. The collapsing bubbles pit the metal, vibrate the set and reduce the efficiency and output.'),
      P('Define the Thoma cavitation factor of a turbine.', 'σ = (Hb − Hs − pv/γ)/H, where Hb is the barometric head, Hs the suction head and pv the vapour-pressure head at the runner.', 'σ = (Hb − Hs − pv/γ)/H.', 'The factor measures the pressure margin above vapour pressure as a fraction of the working head. Every runner has a critical factor σc; cavitation starts when the plant factor falls below it, and the faster runners demand the bigger margins.'),
      P('How is the setting height of a reaction turbine determined?', 'Held so Hs ≤ Hb − pv/γ − σc·H, keeping the plant cavitation factor above the critical value.', 'Hs = Hb − σc·H − pv/γ.', 'Kaplan and high-speed Francis runners need a large critical factor and must therefore sit deep below the tail race. Low-speed machines need a small factor and can stand above the tail level.'),
      P('Compare an impulse turbine with a reaction turbine.', 'An impulse wheel converts the whole head into jet energy in a nozzle and runs at atmospheric pressure; a reaction runner is submerged and the pressure falls across its moving blades.', 'Nozzle action versus blade-passage action.', 'Impulse machines need no pressure casing or draft tube and suit high heads with low discharge; reaction machines fill the casing and recover exit energy with a draft tube over the medium and low head range.'),
      P('Compare the part-load behaviour of the three turbine families.', 'A Kaplan turbine stays efficient over the widest range, a Francis turbine retains a fair range, and a Pelton wheel is efficient only when the jet still fills its buckets.', 'Kaplan widest, Pelton narrowest.', 'Adjustable blades let Kaplan machines track the load; fixed Francis runners suffer moderate shock losses away from the design point; a Pelton wheel run below its design discharge loses efficiency because the shrunken jet no longer fills the buckets.'),
      P('What is the best efficiency point of a turbine?', 'The combination of head, discharge and speed at which the machine gives its maximum efficiency.', 'The design duty point.', 'Every runner has one design point where the guide angle, the blade incidence and the exit swirl are ideal and the losses are minimum. The characteristic curves are used to place the actual site duty as close as possible to this point.'),
      P('State the working principle and main parts of a centrifugal pump.', 'A rotating impeller gives the water kinetic energy and the casing converts it into pressure; the main parts are the impeller, casing, suction pipe, delivery pipe, shaft and bearings.', 'Impeller energy, casing pressure.', 'Water enters the eye of the impeller, is flung outward by the curved vanes and is slowed in the spiral casing to build a pressure rise. The machine delivers a continuous steady flow, unlike a piston pump.'),
      P('Why is a centrifugal pump primed before starting?', 'To fill the suction pipe, casing and impeller with liquid and remove all air, because the impeller cannot create the suction needed to lift water through air.', 'All air expelled before start.', 'With air in the casing the impeller only churns it and no vacuum forms at the eye, so no water rises. Priming is done by flooding from a header tank or with a built-in self-priming device.'),
      P('Define static suction head, static delivery head and static head of a pump.', 'Static suction head is the vertical distance from the sump level to the pump axis, static delivery head from the axis to the delivery level, and the static head is their sum.', 'Hst = hs + hd.', 'These heights depend only on the water levels and the position of the pump. The manometric head always exceeds the static head by the suction and delivery friction losses and the velocity head.'),
      P('What is the manometric head of a centrifugal pump?', 'The total head the pump must supply: Hm = Hs + Hd + hfs + hfd + Vd²/(2g).', 'Hm = Hs + Hd + friction + Vd²/2g.', 'It is the difference between the total heads at the delivery and the suction points and includes the pipe friction and the delivery velocity head. The duty and the power of the pump are calculated on this head.'),
      P('What work is done per second by a pump impeller?', 'W = ρQ(Vw2u2) for radial entry with no inlet whirl, Vw1 = 0.', 'W = ρQ·Vw2u2.', 'The angular-momentum theorem gives the torque as ρQ r2Vw2, and multiplying by the angular velocity gives ρQ(Vw2u2). Real machines need more shaft input because of hydraulic and mechanical losses.'),
      P('Write the theoretical head developed by a pump impeller.', 'He = Vw2u2/g for radial entry, or (Vw2u2 − Vw1u1)/g in general.', 'He = Vw2u2/g.', 'The Euler head comes from dividing the specific work by g. The actual manometric head is lower because of circulatory slip in the vane passages and the hydraulic losses, so the efficiency of the machine compares the two.'),
      P('Describe the velocity triangles at the inlet and outlet of a pump impeller.', 'At inlet the flow is radial with a negligible whirl; at outlet the absolute velocity is the resultant of the peripheral speed u2, the radial flow velocity Vf2 and the relative velocity along the blade.', 'Vw2 = u2 − Vf2 cotβ2.', 'The inlet blade angle is set for shock-free radial entry so Vw1 ≈ 0. At the outlet the tangential component Vw2 = u2 − Vf2 cotβ2 is the quantity that enters the Euler equation for the head.'),
      P('Which impeller vane shape is preferred and why?', 'Backward-curved vanes, because the power rises gently with discharge and the pump does not overload its driver.', 'Backward-curved blades.', 'Forward-curved vanes give a higher head at a given speed but their power steepens sharply with discharge; backward-curved vanes produce a flat stable power curve and the best overall efficiency, so they dominate commercial pumps.'),
      P('Write the discharge through a centrifugal pump impeller.', 'Q = πD2b2Vf2, the product of the outlet circumference, the outlet width and the flow velocity.', 'Q = π D2 b2 Vf2.', 'The flow leaves through the peripheral annulus of area πD2b2 normal to the radial velocity Vf2. It combines with the outlet velocity triangle to fix the blade outlet angle for the chosen duty.'),
      P('Define the coefficient of discharge of a pump impeller.', 'Cd = Q(actual)/Q(theoretical), the ratio of the delivered flow to the flow computed from the velocity diagram.', 'Cd = Qa/Qt.', 'Leakage past the wear rings and imperfect entry make the actual flow smaller than the ideal value. The coefficient lies close to 0.95 for a well-made impeller and corrects the sizing of the eye and the vanes.'),
      P('Define the hydraulic efficiency of a centrifugal pump.', 'ηh = Hm/He, the manometric head divided by the theoretical Euler head.', 'ηh = Hm/He.', 'It measures the friction and shock losses inside the impeller passages and the casing. Large pumps reach about 0.85 to 0.92, and the value depends strongly on how close the working point sits to the design discharge.'),
      P('Define the manometric efficiency of a centrifugal pump.', 'ηman = gHm/(Vw2u2), the useful manometric head expressed as a fraction of the velocity-diagram head.', 'ηman = gHm/(Vw2u2).', 'It compares the actual head output with the theoretical head of the Euler equation, absorbing slip, friction and shock together. Typical values lie between 0.75 and 0.85 for ordinary pumps.'),
      P('Define the mechanical efficiency of a centrifugal pump.', 'ηm = impeller power/shaft power, the fraction of the shaft input that reaches the impeller.', 'ηm = impeller work/shaft work.', 'Bearing, gland and disc friction of the rotating impeller consume part of the input. Mechanical efficiency runs about 0.90 to 0.97 and is the least important of the three factors in a well-built machine.'),
      P('Define the overall efficiency of a centrifugal pump.', 'ηo = ρgQHm/P(shaft), the ratio of the useful hydraulic power to the shaft power.', 'ηo = ρgQHm/P(shaft).', 'It is the product of the hydraulic, volumetric and mechanical efficiencies and expresses the total conversion of shaft input into pumping energy. Large installations reach about 0.80 to 0.88 at the best point.'),
      P('What is the volumetric efficiency of a centrifugal pump?', 'ηv = Q/Qi, the ratio of the delivered discharge to the flow entering the impeller, excluding the leakage past the wear rings.', 'ηv = Q/Qi.', 'Some water recirculates through the narrow gap between the impeller and the casing. Volumetric efficiency multiplies the hydraulic and mechanical efficiencies to form the overall efficiency of the pump.'),
      P('List the losses in a centrifugal pump.', 'Hydraulic losses from friction and shock, volumetric leakage past the wear rings, and mechanical losses in the bearings and gland.', 'Hydraulic, volumetric and mechanical.', 'The three groups multiply together to give the overall efficiency. Shock losses grow very fast away from the design discharge, which is why every pump has one clearly best operating point.'),
      P('What is the minimum starting speed of a centrifugal pump?', 'The lowest speed at which the pressure developed by the impeller overcomes the static head so that delivery begins.', 'Impeller head must exceed the static head.', 'At start the casing water is simply churned until the developed pressure, which grows with the square of the speed, reaches the static head. This minimum speed rises with the static head and falls with the impeller diameter.'),
      P('Name the types of casings used for centrifugal pumps.', 'The volute (spiral) casing, the vortex casing and the diffuser casing fitted with guide vanes.', 'Volute, vortex and diffuser.', 'The widening volute gradually converts velocity into pressure; the diffuser type collects the flow in fixed guide vanes whose enlarging passages give a smoother, more efficient conversion, especially in multistage machines.'),
      P('What are the constructional types of a pump impeller?', 'The closed (enclosed), semi-open and open impellers.', 'Closed, semi-open, open.', 'A closed impeller carries the vanes between two shrouds and gives the best efficiency with clean liquids. Semi-open and open impellers have one shroud or none and are used where dirty or fibrous liquids might clog the passage.'),
      P('Why are centrifugal pump stages arranged in series?', 'To develop a very high head at the same discharge as a single stage.', 'Heads add, discharge is common.', 'Each stage adds its manometric head, so n identical stages give about n times the head of one stage. Boiler-feed and deep-well pumps use this arrangement to reach high delivery pressures.'),
      P('Why are centrifugal pumps arranged in parallel?', 'To supply a large total discharge when the head is limited by the installation.', 'Discharges add at the same head.', 'Several pumps feed a common delivery main, so the flows add while the head stays that of one pump. Machines are started one by one as the demand grows, keeping each unit near its best efficiency point.'),
      P('Write the specific speed of a pump and its use.', 'Ns = N√Q/H^(3/4), each numerical value characterising one impeller shape.', 'Ns = N√Q/H^0.75.', 'Low specific speeds require narrow radial high-head impellers, medium values the mixed-flow shapes, and high values the axial propeller pump for low heads and large discharges. The value is the starting point of impeller selection.'),
      P('What is NPSH and why is it important for a pump?', 'The Net Positive Suction Head is the suction head at the eye above vapour pressure: NPSH(a) = pa/γ − hs − hfs − pv/γ.', 'NPSH(a) = pa/γ − hs − hfs − pv/γ.', 'The available value is set by the installation and must exceed the value required by the impeller at the duty point. If it does not, cavitation begins at the eye, the head and efficiency fall and the impeller is pitted.'),
      P('State the affinity laws of a centrifugal pump.', 'At constant impeller diameter, Q ∝ N, H ∝ N² and P ∝ N³ when the speed changes.', 'Q∝N, H∝N², P∝N³.', 'A small speed change moves the discharge linearly, the head quadratically and the power cubically, so the motor load responds strongly to the coupling ratio. A similar set of laws applies when the impeller diameter changes at constant speed.'),
      P('How is the working point of a pump found?', 'By intersecting the pump head-discharge curve with the system resistance curve of the pipework.', 'Pump curve crosses the system curve.', 'For each discharge the system curve adds the static head to the friction losses that grow with Q². The crossing fixes the actual head and flow, and the pump efficiency at that point decides the running duty.'),
      P('What happens when a centrifugal pump runs at shut-off?', 'At zero discharge the head rises to its shut-off value, the flow recirculates inside the casing and the water heats up.', 'Zero-flow head with heating.', 'With no flow to carry away the energy, the churned water warms until the vapour point may be reached, so prolonged running at shut-off is dangerous. Minimum-flow bypass lines protect large pumps against this condition.'),
      P('How does cavitation affect the performance of a pump?', 'It lowers the head and efficiency, produces noise and vibration, and pits the impeller and casing metal.', 'Head loss, noise and erosion.', 'Vapour cavities at the eye break up the smooth entry flow and collapse with local pressure surges. Continued running erodes the metal, so the suction lift and the speed are limited to keep the available NPSH safe.'),
      P('What factors govern the selection of a centrifugal pump?', 'The discharge, the manometric head, the speed, the NPSH available at the site and the nature of the liquid handled.', 'Duty, speed, NPSH and fluid.', 'The pump is chosen so its best-efficiency point sits at the required duty and its required NPSH stays below the available value at all conditions. The impeller and casing materials then follow the corrosiveness and the temperature of the liquid.'),
      P('How does circulatory slip reduce the head of a pump below the Euler value?', 'A secondary eddy circulation inside the vane passages lowers the effective whirl component at the outlet.', 'Slip cuts the whirl Vw2.', 'The fluid does not follow the blade angle exactly, so the tangential velocity at the exit is reduced by about 10 per cent. This slip factor multiplies the Euler head to give the real theoretical head, and the remaining losses bring it down to the manometric head.'),
      P('Why is pump specific speed based on discharge and turbine specific speed on power?', 'A pump is rated on the flow it delivers (√Q) while a turbine is rated on the output it produces (√P).', 'Flow for pumps, power for turbines.', 'The formula must contain the quantity that fixes the size of the runner or impeller for the duty. Because the two definitions differ, the numerical values of pump and turbine specific speeds cannot be compared directly.'),
      P('State the working principle and main parts of a reciprocating pump.', 'A piston moving in a cylinder alternately sucks and forces the liquid; the main parts are the cylinder, piston, suction and delivery valves, crank, connecting rod and the pipes.', 'Positive displacement by a piston.', 'On the suction stroke the outward piston lowers the cylinder pressure below the sump so the delivery valve closes and water enters through the suction valve; on the delivery stroke the pressure rises, the suction valve shuts and water is forced out at high pressure with a small discharge.'),
      P('Write the discharge of a single-acting reciprocating pump.', 'Q = ALN/60, with A the piston area, L the stroke and N the speed in rpm.', 'Q = ALN/60.', 'The piston discharges only on the forward stroke, once per revolution, so the water volume per minute is the swept volume AL times the speed. The delivery is steady only if an air vessel smooths the flow.'),
      P('Write the discharge of a double-acting reciprocating pump.', 'Q = 2ALN/60, because both forward and return strokes deliver.', 'Q = 2ALN/60.', 'With the rod volume ignored, both faces deliver a swept volume per revolution, doubling the discharge. The flow still pulsates, and air vessels are used to equalise the delivery.'),
      P('What is the slip of a reciprocating pump?', 'Slip = theoretical discharge − actual discharge, and the coefficient of discharge is the ratio of the actual to the theoretical discharge.', 'Cd = Qa/Qt; slip = Qt − Qa.', 'Slip arises from leakage past the piston and valves and from air trapped in the water. The coefficient of discharge therefore lies slightly below unity in normal operation.'),
      P('When does a reciprocating pump show negative slip?', 'When the actual discharge exceeds the theoretical value, which happens at high speed when the momentum of the delivery column keeps the delivery valve open during part of the suction stroke.', 'Actual flow greater than theoretical.', 'At high speeds the pressure on the delivery side stays high during the stroke change, so some water continues to leave through the delivery valve while the suction valve opens. Negative slip is thus a speed-dependent anomaly of the simple theory.'),
      P('Write the work done per second by a single-acting reciprocating pump.', 'W = ρgQ(Hs + Hd) = ρgALN(Hs + Hd)/60, where the heads include the suction and delivery heights.', 'W = ρgQ(Hs + Hd).', 'Ignoring acceleration and friction, the piston must raise each volume of water through the sum of the suction and delivery heads. Multiplying by the discharge gives the hydraulic power; the input power is larger by the losses and the mechanical efficiency.'),
      P('Write the work done by a double-acting reciprocating pump.', 'W = 2ρgALN(Hs + Hd)/60, twice the single-acting value for the same stroke and speed.', 'W = 2ρgQ(Hs + Hd).', 'Both strokes deliver, so the flow and hence the work double when the rod volume is ignored. The formula assumes no acceleration or pipe friction; indicator diagrams include those terms.'),
      P('What does the indicator diagram of a reciprocating pump record?', 'The plot of the cylinder pressure against the piston stroke, showing the suction and delivery lines and the losses.', 'Pressure versus stroke trace.', 'The closed loop is drawn by an indicator attached to the cylinder. Its area multiplied by the cylinder scale gives the work done per cycle, and the departures of the suction and delivery lines from the ideal show the effect of acceleration and friction in the pipes.'),
      P('How does the acceleration of the piston affect the indicator diagram?', 'It tilts the suction and delivery lines because the accelerating liquid needs extra pressure at the start of each stroke and releases it at the end.', 'Inertia head tilts the lines.', 'The water column accelerates and decelerates twice per revolution, adding an inertia head that raises the required pressure at the start and lowers it at the end of each stroke. The diagram leans accordingly, and near the stroke ends the suction pressure may fall to the separation head.'),
      P('How does pipe friction affect the indicator diagram?', 'It raises the delivery-pressure line and depresses the suction line at every stroke position, widening the loop.', 'Friction lifts and lowers the lines.', 'Friction loss is proportional to the square of the velocity, so it is greatest at mid-stroke where the piston speed is highest. The indicator diagram grows wider, the work per cycle rises and the pump efficiency falls.'),
      P('What is the function and position of an air vessel on a reciprocating pump?', 'It is a closed tank partially filled with air, fitted close to the cylinder on the suction and delivery pipes to smooth the flow.', 'One near the pump on each side.', 'The air cushion compresses as water enters and expands as the pump flow varies, so a nearly uniform velocity replaces the pulsating stream. Uniform flow cuts the acceleration head and the friction loss, saving a large fraction of the pump work.'),
      P('How much work does an air vessel save in a reciprocating pump?', 'Freeing the pipe flow from pulsation removes almost all the acceleration work and about 85 per cent of the friction work.', 'Friction down to near the uniform-flow value.', 'With an air vessel the water in the long pipe moves at a nearly constant speed, so the velocity-dependent losses shrink to the low-mean-flow values. The saving depends on the position of the vessel and is largest when it sits close to the cylinder on a long delivery line.'),
      P('What is separation in the suction of a reciprocating pump?', 'The breaking of the water column when the pressure in the cylinder falls to the vapour pressure of the liquid.', 'Suction pressure near vapour pressure.', 'During the suction stroke the inertia and friction heads reduce the cylinder pressure; at the vapour-pressure head of about 2.5 m of water the liquid vaporises and the column separates. Keeping the suction lift below about 7 to 8 m with short straight pipes avoids this condition.'),
      P('Compare a centrifugal pump with a reciprocating pump.', 'A centrifugal pump gives a steady high discharge at low to medium pressure and needs priming; a reciprocating pump delivers a low but very high-pressure flow and is self-priming.', 'High flow versus high pressure.', 'Centrifugal machines are cheap, compact, easily governed and handle dirty liquids but cannot lift from deep wells; reciprocating machines hold a constant pressure, suit boiler feed and oil service, but are bulky, slow and pulsating. Centrifugal pumps have displaced reciprocating types for most water duties.'),
      P('What is a hydraulic ram?', 'A device that lifts a small quantity of water to a great height by using the water hammer produced when a large low-head supply flow is suddenly stopped by a valve.', 'Water hammer drives the lift.', 'The flowing supply closes the waste valve and the resulting pressure surge opens a delivery valve into an air chamber from which a small high-head flow is delivered continuously. Efficiencies reach about 60 to 70 per cent when the lift ratio is moderate.'),
      P('What is a hydraulic accumulator?', 'A pressure reservoir that stores hydraulic energy, formed by a cylinder with a loaded ram whose weight holds the fluid under constant pressure.', 'Weight-loaded energy storage.', 'The ram rises as the pump delivers surplus fluid, and its weight keeps the system pressure constant while the stored fluid flows out during peak demand. It smooths the pump load and buffers pressure shocks in presses and testing rigs.'),
      P('What is a hydraulic intensifier?', 'A device that raises the pressure of a low-pressure fluid supply by applying its force on a large piston to a smaller piston on the same ram.', 'Force on a large area, pressure on a small one.', 'The pressure ratio equals the large area divided by the small area, so a 5:1 area ratio multiplies the pressure fivefold. It is used to drive testing machines, presses and other equipment needing a pressure above the pump rating.'),
      P('Describe a hydraulic lift used for raising loads.', 'A machine that raises a load by the direct action of pressure on a ram, with a small pump supplying the high-pressure fluid.', 'Direct pressure lift of the ram.', 'The oil pressure acts over the ram area to lift a load equal to pA, so a moderate pressure raises a heavy car or goods platform. The pump supply sets the speed, and the weight of the ram and load balances the pressure.'),
      P('What is a hydraulic crane?', 'A crane whose jib is raised and swung by hydraulic cylinders instead of ropes and drums.', 'Hydraulic actuation of the jib.', 'High-pressure fluid extends the cylinders to raise the jib and slews the turntable smoothly, giving precise control and a high load capability in a compact machine. The cylinder force equals the pressure times the piston area.'),
      P('State the principle of the hydraulic press.', 'Pascal law: the pressure generated by a small force on a small piston acts undiminished and is multiplied over the large platen area to give a great force.', 'F2 = F1 × (A2/A1).', 'A small ram builds a high oil pressure that acts equally on the large platen, so the machine multiplies the force by the area ratio. Hydraulic presses are used for forging, forming, baling and forcing operations.'),
      P('What is a fluid coupling?', 'A hydrodynamic coupling with an impeller and a runner in a closed casing that transmits torque through swirling oil without mechanical contact.', 'Torque transfer by oil momentum.', 'The impeller throws the oil outward and the runner receives its momentum, transmitting nearly the same torque at both ends. The slip between the speeds is small at normal loads, and the coupling absorbs shock and torsional vibration between a prime mover and its driven machine.'),
      P('How does a torque converter differ from a fluid coupling?', 'A torque converter adds a fixed reactor or stator between the runner and the impeller so that the output torque can exceed the input torque.', 'A stator multiplies the torque.', 'The stator redirects the oil leaving the runner back into the impeller, increasing the change of momentum and hence the torque over a range of speed ratios. The multiplication falls as the output speed rises, giving automatic matching to the load, and a lock-up clutch may later short-circuit the unit.'),
      P('Name the common types of water wheels.', 'The overshot, breastshot and undershot wheels.', 'Three wheel classes.', 'An undershot wheel is driven by the velocity of the water below it, a breastshot wheel by the water entering near its axle, and an overshot wheel by the weight of water falling into buckets over the top. The overshot type is the most efficient of the three but needs a fair vertical fall.'),
      P('Compare the overshot wheel with the undershot wheel.', 'The overshot wheel uses the weight of the water in top-loaded buckets and reaches about 60–70 per cent efficiency, while the undershot wheel uses only the jet momentum and reaches about 20–30 per cent.', 'Weight action versus momentum action.', 'An overshot wheel can run with a small discharge and a good fall, while an undershot wheel works on a large flow with a low fall and poor efficiency. Water wheels, being slow and bulky, were eventually superseded by the faster hydraulic turbines at higher powers.'),
    ];
  if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) module.exports = SSC_JE_ENC_FLUIDS;
  if (typeof window !== "undefined") window.SSC_JE_ENC_FLUIDS = SSC_JE_ENC_FLUIDS;
})();